```json
{
    "chapters": [
        {
            "title": "Python Proficiency for the Deep Learning Workflow",
            "description": "Master the Python constructs and paradigms used daily in PyTorch development. Covers essential data structures, control flow, function definition, and comprehensions. Introduces the principles of Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)—focusing on classes, methods, and inheritance—as this is the foundation of PyTorch's `nn.Module`."
        },
        {
            "title": "Numerical Computing with NumPy",
            "description": "Build fluency in the scientific toolkit that PyTorch extends. Master NumPy's n-dimensional arrays as the conceptual precursor to PyTorch tensors. Covers creation, indexing, reshaping, broadcasting, and vectorized operations for efficient numerical computation without explicit loops."
        },
        {
            "title": "Linear Algebra - The Framework for Data & Models",
            "description": "Establish the mathematical language of deep learning. Learn vectors and matrices as data structures, and master core operations: addition, multiplication (dot product and element-wise), transposition, and norms. Introduce tensors as multi-dimensional arrays and explore key concepts like rank, eigenvalues, and eigenvectors with a focus on their geometric intuition and role in data transformations."
        },
        {
            "title": "Calculus - Understanding Change & Optimization",
            "description": "Develop the conceptual foundation for how neural networks learn. Understand derivatives, partial derivatives, and gradients as tools to measure rates of change. Master the chain rule as the critical mechanism for training. Focus on interpreting the meaning of gradients, not on performing manual symbolic differentiation."
        },
        {
            "title": "Probability & Statistics - Modeling Data and Uncertainty",
            "description": "Learn to characterize data and formalize uncertainty, which underpins model evaluation and output interpretation. Covers descriptive statistics (mean, variance), fundamental probability concepts (independence, expectation), and key distributions (Normal, Bernoulli). Introduces Bayes' Theorem and maximum likelihood estimation as core statistical viewpoints in machine learning."
        },
        {
            "title": "Synthesis Project - Building the Engine Core from Scratch",
            "description": "Integrate mathematical concepts and numerical programming to manually construct the core mechanisms of deep learning. Using only Python and NumPy, implement a multi-layer perceptron, code forward and backward passes (applying the chain rule manually), and run a full gradient descent optimization loop. This cements the foundational theory before leveraging PyTorch's automation."
        }
    ]
}
```

```json
{
    "chapters": [
        {
            "title": "The Tensor Core - PyTorch's Fundamental Data Structure",
            "description": "Master the creation, manipulation, and properties of PyTorch tensors. Learn to initialize tensors from various sources, perform core arithmetic and linear algebra operations, manage tensor shape with methods like `view` and `squeeze`, and control critical attributes like `dtype` and `device` for CPU/GPU management."
        },
        {
            "title": "The Engine of Learning - Automatic Differentiation with Autograd",
            "description": "Delve into PyTorch's automatic differentiation system (`autograd`). Understand the `requires_grad` flag, compute gradients using `.backward()`, and learn to control gradient flow for tensors. Practice calculating gradients for scalars and multi-dimensional tensors to build the foundation for network training."
        },
        {
            "title": "Data Pipeline Engineering - Datasets and DataLoaders",
            "description": "Learn to build efficient data input pipelines. Implement custom datasets by subclassing `torch.utils.data.Dataset`, utilize built-in datasets, and create `DataLoader` instances for automatic batching, shuffling, and parallel loading. Apply tensor transformations to preprocess data on the fly."
        },
        {
            "title": "Architectural Blueprints - Building Networks with `nn.Module`",
            "description": "Construct neural network architectures by subclassing `nn.Module`. Define layers (e.g., Linear, Dropout) in `__init__` and orchestrate the data flow in the `forward` method. Learn to apply activation functions from `torch.nn.functional` and organize layers using containers like `nn.Sequential`."
        },
        {
            "title": "The Training Loop - Loss, Optimizers, and Gradient Descent",
            "description": "Assemble the core iterative training mechanism. Select and apply loss functions (e.g., `MSELoss`, `CrossEntropyLoss`), configure optimizers (e.g., `SGD`, `Adam`) with the model's parameters, and manually code the training loop: zeroing gradients, forward pass, loss computation, backward pass, and the optimizer step."
        },
        {
            "title": "Model Evaluation - Metrics, Validation, and Preventing Overfit",
            "description": "Implement the model evaluation protocol. Learn to switch between training and evaluation modes using `.train()` and `.eval()`, disable gradient tracking with `torch.no_grad()` for efficient inference, calculate performance metrics (like accuracy), and integrate validation within the training cycle to monitor for overfitting."
        },
        {
            "title": "Deployment Fundamentals - Saving, Loading, and Running Inference",
            "description": "Complete the development pipeline by learning model persistence. Save and load full model architectures or just the learned parameter state (`state_dict`). Practice running inference (making predictions) on new data with a loaded model to validate the end-to-end workflow from training to application."
        }
    ]
}
```

```json
{
    "chapters": [
        {
            "title": "The Machine Learning Landscape & Foundational Paradigms",
            "description": "Establish the core problem setting and vocabulary of machine learning. Cover supervised vs. unsupervised vs. reinforcement learning, the structure of a dataset (features, labels), and the critical concepts of training, validation, and test splits. Introduce the central challenge of generalization and the bias-variance tradeoff, framing the entire goal of model training."
        },
        {
            "title": "The Mathematical Engine Room: Linear Regression & Gradient Descent",
            "description": "Build the first complete, understandable model. Derive simple linear regression from scratch, framing it as a parametric model. Define the Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss function. Introduce the core concept of optimization via Gradient Descent, manually calculating the gradient of the loss with respect to the model's weights and implementing the weight update rule in NumPy."
        },
        {
            "title": "From Linear Model to Artificial Neuron: The Perceptron",
            "description": "Bridge the gap from linear regression to neural networks. Introduce the perceptron model for binary classification, highlighting the critical addition of a non-linear activation function (e.g., step, sigmoid). Discuss the concept and limitation of linear separability. Implement a perceptron's forward pass using NumPy."
        },
        {
            "title": "Building Depth: Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs) & Activation Functions",
            "description": "Explain how stacking layers creates a universal function approximator. Define the architecture of a basic MLP (input, hidden, output layers). Explore the necessity, properties, and behavior of core non-linear activation functions (Sigmoid, Tanh, ReLU). Diagram and manually compute the forward pass of a small 2-layer network using matrix operations in NumPy."
        },
        {
            "title": "The Learning Algorithm: Backpropagation from First Principles",
            "description": "Derive the core algorithm that makes deep learning feasible. Apply the chain rule from calculus to computational graphs. Walk through manually calculating the gradient of a loss function with respect to every weight in a simple network, layer by layer, from the output back to the input. Implement this backward pass for a small network in NumPy to solidify understanding of gradient flow."
        },
        {
            "title": "A Toolkit of Loss Functions",
            "description": "Deepen the understanding of how task error is quantified. Formally define and contrast common regression losses (MSE, MAE) and classification losses (Binary Cross-Entropy, Categorical Cross-Entropy). Explain the probabilistic interpretation behind cross-entropy and implement these loss calculations from scratch in NumPy."
        },
        {
            "title": "Beyond Vanilla: Advanced Optimization & Optimizers",
            "description": "Address the practical shortcomings of basic Gradient Descent. Introduce the core ideas behind advanced optimizers: Momentum (handling ravines), RMSProp (adaptive learning rates), and Adam (combining concepts). Explain their intuition and update rules in plain terms, and implement a basic version of Momentum within a NumPy training loop."
        },
        {
            "title": "Fighting Overfitting: Core Regularization Techniques",
            "description": "Address the central problem of overfitting and how to combat it. Explain and motivate common regularization techniques: L1/L2 weight decay (as a penalty term added to the loss) and Dropout (conceptual mechanism and training vs. inference behavior). Discuss their effect on the model's weight distribution and generalization."
        },
        {
            "title": "Measuring Success: Model Evaluation & Core Metrics",
            "description": "Define the framework for assessing model performance fairly and quantitatively. Detail the calculation, interpretation, and use case for key evaluation metrics: for classification (Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-Score) and for regression (R-squared, Mean Absolute Error). Write code to calculate these metrics from predictions and true labels using NumPy."
        }
    ]
}
```

