```json
{
    "levels": [
        {
            "title": "Scalars and Vectors - The Foundational Quantities",
            "description": "Establish the atomic units. Define scalars as single numbers (magnitudes) and vectors as ordered lists of numbers representing points or directions in space. Learn vector notation, properties, and the core operations of addition and scalar multiplication, building geometric intuition for translation and scaling."
        },
        {
            "title": "Matrices - Representing Tabular Data and Linear Maps",
            "description": "Introduce matrices as two-dimensional rectangular arrays. Understand them as structures for storing datasets (e.g., feature matrices) and as representations of linear transformations. Learn matrix notation, indexing, and the fundamental operations of addition, scalar multiplication, and matrix-vector multiplication as transforming a vector's space."
        },
        {
            "title": "Core Operations - Addition, Transposition, and Element-wise Rules",
            "description": "Master the essential arithmetic for vectors and matrices. Perform element-wise addition and multiplication (the Hadamard product). Learn the critical operation of transposition (swapping rows and columns) and its implications for data organization and preparing operands for further computation."
        },
        {
            "title": "Composition and Projection - The Dot Product and Matrix Multiplication",
            "description": "Deeply explore combinatorial operations. Define the dot product for vectors and generalize it to matrix multiplication. Learn the precise rules governing these operations (dimensionality compatibility). Build geometric intuition, interpreting them as computing projections, similarity (cosine), and the sequential application of linear transformations."
        },
        {
            "title": "Norms, Distances, and the Geometry of Data",
            "description": "Quantify the geometry of vector spaces. Define vector norms (L1, L2) as measures of magnitude. Use norms to calculate distances (e.g., Euclidean) between data points. Formalize the geometric interpretations of vector operations and introduce cosine similarity, connecting the dot product to the angle between vectors."
        },
        {
            "title": "Matrix Properties - Rank, Inverses, and Solving Linear Systems",
            "description": "Investigate properties that reveal the structure and solvability of linear systems. Define matrix rank as the number of linearly independent rows/columns, indicating the intrinsic dimensionality of a transformation or dataset. Introduce identity matrices, inverses, and frame solving a set of linear equations as a matrix equation, laying groundwork for parameter estimation."
        },
        {
            "title": "Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors - Characterizing Transformations",
            "description": "Uncover the fundamental axes and scaling factors of a linear transformation. Define eigenvectors (directionally invariant vectors) and eigenvalues (their scaling factors). Explore their geometric meaning, computational derivation, and paramount importance in data science for understanding stability, oscillations, and dimensionality reduction (e.g., Principal Component Analysis)."
        },
        {
            "title": "Tensors - The N-Dimensional Data Structure of Deep Learning",
            "description": "Generalize all prior concepts to the core data structure. Define a tensor by its rank (number of axes/indices) and shape. Understand how scalars (rank-0), vectors (rank-1), and matrices (rank-2) are specific tensor types. Learn how complex, real-world data (batched images, time-series, embeddings) is naturally represented as higher-rank tensors, completing the framework."
        }
    ]
}
```

