            "description": "This level establishes the core architectural pattern of a Convolutional Neural Network. You will learn that a CNN is built by stacking three fundamental types of layers: Convolutional layers (to detect features), Activation functions (to introduce non-linearity), and Pooling layers (to downsample and add invariance). The level outlines the purpose of each block and introduces the standard sequence that forms a basic CNN building block."
        },
        {
            "title": "The Convolutional Layer - Feature Extraction with Learnable Filters",
            "description": "This level introduces the core operation of a CNN. You will learn how a convolutional layer uses a set of small, learnable filters (or kernels) to systematically scan an input image or feature map. The focus is on understanding how each filter detects specific local patterns (like edges or textures) by computing element-wise products and sums, producing a 2D activation map. You will learn the role of key parameters: the number of filters (which defines the output depth/channels) and the concept of local receptive fields."
        },
        {
            "title": "Controlling Output Dimensions: Stride and Padding",
            "description": "This level covers the essential hyperparameters that control the spatial dimensions of the convolution operation. You will learn how 'stride' determines the step size of the filter, affecting downsampling, and how 'padding' (adding borders of zeros) is used to preserve spatial size or control information loss at the edges. The level concludes with the formula to calculate the output spatial dimensions given input size, kernel size, padding, and stride."
        },
